WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.395
I need you to drop the
plane to 8,000 feet.

00:00:05.820 --> 00:00:07.410
It is possible, yes,

00:00:07.410 --> 00:00:10.020
to come down like a rock, really fast.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:12.840
We are trained to do that
in case of severe emergency.

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.880
Hi, my name is Capt. Zoya Agarwal.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:18.990
I am a commander on the
Boeing 777 with Air India,

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.350
and I have been doing this
for the last 18 years.

00:00:22.350 --> 00:00:24.750
Today, we are going to be
looking at pilot emergencies

00:00:24.750 --> 00:00:28.470
in movies and judge how real they are.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:29.820
We're in an uncontrolled dive,

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.860
descending out of 21,000 feet.

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.810
Got a jammed stabilizer or something.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.256
They are in an uncontrollable dive,

00:00:35.256 --> 00:00:37.170
but they also have a problem
with their stabilizer,

00:00:37.170 --> 00:00:39.600
which basically keeps the airplane

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.220
stable in the pitch control.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:44.181
Pitch means vertically, to go up and down.

00:00:44.181 --> 00:00:45.394
Evans, listen to me.

00:00:45.394 --> 00:00:47.430
When I say I want you
to retract the flaps,

00:00:47.430 --> 00:00:50.220
retract the gear, trim us nose down.

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:51.360
Why would I trim down?

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:53.310
This is not how it's supposed to be.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:58.170
As a pilot, you are
ingrained with this thought

00:00:58.170 --> 00:00:59.310
from the very beginning.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:00.450
Whenever something goes wrong,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:03.240
pick up the quick-reference
handbook, which has a list

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:05.580
of all the potential
failures, including this.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.950
I can command my copilot
to pull the checklist.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.080
I would follow that, realistically.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.050
He's trying to trim down, he's
trying to get the flaps out,

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:14.310
he's trying to get the gear out.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.020
I don't know what he's trying to do.

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:19.110
For me, it's a very huge
walk outside the box,

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.640
which I would not step out of.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.590
When I tell you I want
you to push these forward,

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.424
full power, full
throttle. Can you do that?

00:01:24.424 --> 00:01:25.257
Flight attendant: Yeah.

00:01:25.257 --> 00:01:27.720
You will never call a flight attendant

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:30.407
into the flight deck to help you to fly

00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:32.700
when both of you are alive.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.040
You have a two-man flight deck.

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:36.870
One is a captain and one is a copilot,

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:38.190
and that's all that you need.

00:01:38.190 --> 00:01:39.990
So it's absolutely unrealistic.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.707
Here we go. I've got control.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.380
This is based on a real-life incident,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.160
the Alaska Air flight 261 in January 2000.

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:55.440
This is a very extreme maneuver.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:58.470
I respect, in real life,
whatever captain may have done.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:00.690
The only planes that fly inverted

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:04.290
are acrobats and probably military.

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:08.160
It's an MD-80, and this is not approved

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:10.440
and test-flown for that.

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:14.670
They're supposed to be
secured, our overhead lockers.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:17.040
The manufacturers design
it in the best of the way.

00:02:17.040 --> 00:02:20.250
If, maybe, sometimes
passenger may leave it open,

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:21.504
maybe that's why.

00:02:21.504 --> 00:02:23.654
Radio: 227, it's 1 o'clock, three miles.

00:02:24.632 --> 00:02:27.240
Commendable. We can say that now,

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:29.820
because the flight went
on for two more minutes.

00:02:29.820 --> 00:02:32.870
My assessment of the situation would be

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.502
follow the checklist, do
your best, and that's it.

00:02:37.502 --> 00:02:38.343
Zero.

00:02:38.343 --> 00:02:41.129
Because inverted flight and everything,

00:02:41.129 --> 00:02:43.198
that does not make any sense to me.

00:02:51.720 --> 00:02:54.900
This flight was Sichuan Air
from Chongqing to Lhasa.

00:02:54.900 --> 00:02:56.670
They had a windshield crack,

00:02:56.670 --> 00:02:58.260
and the copilot got sucked out

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:00.360
because of the differential pressure.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.340
He's just trying to come down really fast,

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:04.950
so he could reduce the
differential pressure.

00:03:04.950 --> 00:03:06.480
And the less the differential pressure,

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.012
he could easily pull him
back inside the flight deck.

00:03:09.012 --> 00:03:10.875
That was realistic.

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.310
We have a system called GPWS,

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:20.670
which does not allow us to
fly so close to the terrain,

00:03:20.670 --> 00:03:21.960
like, mountainous region.

00:03:21.960 --> 00:03:26.280
Would have been going all
over the place in real life,

00:03:26.280 --> 00:03:31.280
because it warns you way ahead
of any possible collision.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:36.960
Did you see the squawk 7700?

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:38.580
That is the emergency squawk code.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:41.970
You enter 7700 on the transponder,

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.670
they will move mountains for you,

00:03:44.670 --> 00:03:46.440
the air-traffic controller,
because they know

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.360
that you're dealing with
an emergency situation

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:51.270
and try and help you
out as much as they can.

00:03:51.270 --> 00:03:53.790
No. 2, you saw a cabin crew member

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.850
came inside the flight deck,
the cockpit, to help him out.

00:03:56.850 --> 00:03:58.860
This is the kind of scenario
that would warrant that.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.426
Not when two pilots are awake
and absolutely fine and fit.

00:04:07.050 --> 00:04:10.150
Every storm is different.
The storm, visually,

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:13.290
is something that you would
see on your weather radar.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.200
You would also see what
kind of storm it is,

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.400
its convective activity,

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:18.900
what is happening within the storm.

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:21.120
And after years of experience,

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.600
we know the kind of buildup
that is and how far.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.830
Oh, this was realistic.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:28.290
Except for that thing above the mountain.

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:31.390
So, I think I'll subtract,
like, two marks. 8.

00:04:34.945 --> 00:04:36.030
The hell was that?

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:37.770
This is absolutely not realistic.

00:04:37.770 --> 00:04:41.850
If you were to have damaged
wheels for whatever reason,

00:04:41.850 --> 00:04:43.140
there could be adverse effects.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:45.900
If you remember the Concorde crash,

00:04:45.900 --> 00:04:49.080
where this airplane was taking
off from Paris to New York

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.290
and the debris from the wheel,

00:04:52.290 --> 00:04:56.160
it got inside the fuel
tank and it ruptured it,

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.080
and it kind of just blew up

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.210
the whole Concorde, unfortunately.

00:05:00.210 --> 00:05:03.870
So even a small piece of
debris can have a huge impact.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:04.950
The only thing which made it

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:06.810
more palatable for me is the seat belts.

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.143
People were at least
seated. Thank God for that.

00:05:10.331 --> 00:05:12.330
We're going down! We're going down!

00:05:12.330 --> 00:05:13.163
Sasha: Take over.

00:05:13.163 --> 00:05:15.636
Take over?! What do you
mean, take over? Sasha!

00:05:15.636 --> 00:05:16.890
Sasha: Relax!

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.270
If there were some emergency,

00:05:18.270 --> 00:05:20.880
I would always be in the
flight deck to solve it first

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:22.380
and take it from there.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:31.140
You cannot hit an object with an airplane.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:34.350
Airplanes are very aerodynamically clean,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.940
the way they're supposed to fly.

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:37.200
Do you remember "Sully"?

00:05:37.200 --> 00:05:38.790
Like, something, a small bird,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.597
how it can just take away the engines?

00:05:41.597 --> 00:05:44.790
This is the impact when
you're adding speed

00:05:44.790 --> 00:05:47.640
and a foreign material or foreign body.

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.410
Minus 10.

00:05:49.410 --> 00:05:51.360
Sorry, but what do I do?

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.223
Please show me something realistic!

00:05:54.240 --> 00:05:55.740
Copilot: Mayday, mayday, mayday.

00:05:55.740 --> 00:05:58.110
Mayday. We have lost avionics.

00:05:58.110 --> 00:06:00.420
"Mayday" is definitely
not just in the movies.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.700
There are many ways of
doing different things

00:06:02.700 --> 00:06:04.890
to let the air-traffic controller know

00:06:04.890 --> 00:06:07.380
that you're suffering
an emergent situation.

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:09.540
However, when you say,
"Mayday, mayday, mayday,"

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.090
it definitely just
straightaway attracts attention

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:13.770
and everybody just goes quiet,

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:18.504
because they know that you
require that emergent attention.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:20.825
The hell?!

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:24.390
They only drop when there is actually

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:25.380
a need to be dropped,

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:27.900
like in case of a loss of cabin pressure.

00:06:27.900 --> 00:06:29.490
But this does not happen.

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:31.482
There isn't loss of
cabin pressure over here.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:39.090
Well, of course, I have heard
of having a rat or something.

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:42.960
If one passenger spots one rat,

00:06:42.960 --> 00:06:45.120
that entire airplane is sealed up,

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.150
fumed for a very long time.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:52.080
It's not about an animal,
it's about the damage.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:54.600
Because, if you think about a rat,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.340
it can chew up wires,

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:58.680
which can potentially
control the flight controls.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:00.360
Oh, my God, minus 2.

00:07:00.360 --> 00:07:02.430
I like the fact they were
wearing their uniform,

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:03.264
and that's it.

00:07:05.550 --> 00:07:07.115
It's impossible to land here!

00:07:07.115 --> 00:07:08.515
How much fuel you have, sir?

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:12.090
Only about 920 kg's.

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:13.860
That means hardly one or two attempts.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:14.970
I love Bollywood.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:16.860
They were very, very low on fuel.

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.260
That situation should
never come in aviation,

00:07:19.260 --> 00:07:21.570
because you're dealing with
human lives. You're in air.

00:07:21.570 --> 00:07:25.020
We always have enough
contingency plans in place

00:07:25.020 --> 00:07:28.387
to have enough fuel with us
at any given point in time,

00:07:28.387 --> 00:07:32.790
and if we don't, we initiate our plan B,

00:07:32.790 --> 00:07:35.460
which is a diversion much before.

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.350
Even if you have low fuel,

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:40.290
you don't linger around at a
place which has bad weather.

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:43.493
You go to some other choice to start with.

00:07:43.493 --> 00:07:44.663
Going blindly.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.420
Clear for landing runway 16.

00:07:48.420 --> 00:07:52.320
No. Clear runway 34.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:54.120
Controller: 34 is the
opposite wind pressure!

00:07:54.120 --> 00:07:55.470
Headwind will become tailwind!

00:07:55.470 --> 00:07:57.180
This is a very unrealistic scenario,

00:07:57.180 --> 00:07:59.610
except for the fact that
the air-traffic controllers

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:01.530
were surprised that he
was coming into landing

00:08:01.530 --> 00:08:03.240
with high tailwinds.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.173
What he was trying to say was that,

00:08:05.173 --> 00:08:07.302
typically, if you're
landing, when you go in,

00:08:07.302 --> 00:08:10.898
you should ideally have
winds coming from the head.

00:08:10.898 --> 00:08:12.600
And if you have tailwinds,

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:14.310
which is coming from behind you,

00:08:14.310 --> 00:08:19.260
you tend to touch down a little
bit more into the runway.

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.700
If you're landing in terrible storm,

00:08:20.700 --> 00:08:23.220
well, we have limitations.
We abide by that.

00:08:23.220 --> 00:08:24.810
The air-traffic controller was shocked

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:26.190
because, obviously, the winds

00:08:26.190 --> 00:08:29.490
were probably much, much
more than his limitations.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:30.919
Brace for impact.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:36.540
You know, this is so
weird, because the copilot,

00:08:36.540 --> 00:08:38.580
I mean, I love the fact
they've shown a woman,

00:08:38.580 --> 00:08:41.850
but the copilot has to be assertive.

00:08:41.850 --> 00:08:43.140
My first officers,

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.150
I've always told them,
"You're the second in command.

00:08:45.150 --> 00:08:49.320
This airplane, after me,
is like, you're in charge.

00:08:49.320 --> 00:08:50.640
Always act like one."

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:54.270
It's a part of our crew briefings

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:55.890
that if there is anything

00:08:55.890 --> 00:08:58.200
that you're not comfortable with me doing,

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:00.552
you call that out.

00:09:07.180 --> 00:09:08.610
My God, don't get me started.

00:09:08.610 --> 00:09:11.340
Would you even cross a
road with your eyes closed?

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.350
I mean, you wouldn't even
walk out of this door

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:14.580
with your eyes closed.

00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:16.470
You're dealing with human lives!

00:09:16.470 --> 00:09:17.907
Minus 100.

00:09:18.750 --> 00:09:22.710
Because, blind landing? Oh, my God, no!

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.900
I need you to drop the
plane to 8,000 feet.

00:09:24.900 --> 00:09:27.275
Get to the lowest pressure
differential you can.

00:09:33.900 --> 00:09:35.550
This is definitely more realistic.

00:09:35.550 --> 00:09:37.392
Basically, what he's trying to say is

00:09:37.392 --> 00:09:39.090
in case of a rapid decompression,

00:09:39.090 --> 00:09:42.270
when we have a decompression
within the airplane,

00:09:42.270 --> 00:09:46.440
your inside of the cabin
pressure is really high

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:49.200
and the outside is really low.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.830
So your differential pressure,
or delta P, as we call it,

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:56.580
is higher, which is really detrimental,

00:09:56.580 --> 00:09:58.410
which is why the masks come down.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:09:59.580
Otherwise, you cannot breathe.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:02.940
The pilot put the airplane
in that kind of an attitude

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:06.480
to kind of sabotage the
efforts of the gunman

00:10:06.480 --> 00:10:08.220
and destabilize him in doing

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:09.900
whatever potential damage he was doing.

00:10:09.900 --> 00:10:11.940
So that was a great move.

00:10:11.940 --> 00:10:14.213
Come on, you [beep]!

00:10:16.250 --> 00:10:18.180
It is possible, yes,

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:20.760
to come down like a rock, really fast.

00:10:20.760 --> 00:10:23.460
We are trained to do that
in case of severe emergency.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:25.860
In case of rapid decompression,

00:10:25.860 --> 00:10:28.350
that does require you to
descend really, really fast.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:30.532
Because every single nanosecond that

00:10:30.532 --> 00:10:34.230
you're spending time
wasting or not coming down,

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:37.440
you can potentially have a lot
of damage behind the cabin,

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:39.898
people passing out
because of lack of oxygen.

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.750
So, effect of an explosion is far more.

00:10:45.750 --> 00:10:49.230
Like, it's a depressurization
and, potentially,

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.640
you have a far bigger impact
in terms of the damage.

00:10:53.640 --> 00:10:57.600
We do have protocol that we
follow in case of explosives.

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:00.392
I do not know if I can
openly discuss that out.

00:11:06.998 --> 00:11:10.020
An airplane can have a
partial damage to it,

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:11.610
and it can still very much land back,

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:14.190
so long as your controls are not affected,

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.640
there's no major damage
to the essential services

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:21.300
which are required for the
flight control of the airplane,

00:11:21.300 --> 00:11:22.491
they are fully functioning.

00:11:22.491 --> 00:11:24.341
They're designed to be like that.

00:11:24.341 --> 00:11:25.500
Let's give it a 2.

00:11:25.500 --> 00:11:28.284
I'm feeling good. Generous.

00:11:30.060 --> 00:11:31.244
Shaw: Tell him to take off!

00:11:36.516 --> 00:11:37.920
Oh, my God, I love that part.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.297
Totally a killer.

00:11:40.320 --> 00:11:42.530
You would never just take off on a whim.

00:11:42.530 --> 00:11:43.920
You know, "Hey, I feel like taking off,

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:45.540
and somebody's calling me to take off."

00:11:45.540 --> 00:11:46.710
It doesn't work like that.

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:49.170
The factors of takeoff speed
depends on many things.

00:11:49.170 --> 00:11:51.270
Even if, like, one degree
of temperature change

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.730
or slight wind change or pressure change,

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:56.040
we recalculate our takeoff performance.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:58.530
For example, you're
expecting 300 passengers

00:11:58.530 --> 00:11:59.940
and 100 of them don't show up.

00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.740
That changes the takeoff performance.

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:04.710
We have this figured out way in advance,

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:09.120
and we ensure even if there
is a minute change to it,

00:12:09.120 --> 00:12:11.730
we recalculate everything.

00:12:11.730 --> 00:12:15.150
But, rest assured, it's
never a woman calling us

00:12:15.150 --> 00:12:17.509
on the phone, "Take off, take off!"

00:12:23.606 --> 00:12:26.370
You would never have a car
even close to a runway.

00:12:26.370 --> 00:12:30.000
It's all very sterile environment
in real-life airports.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:33.600
What you're essentially doing
is there's drag being added,

00:12:33.600 --> 00:12:37.080
which is pulling it down
along with the weight.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.247
And when you're taking off
a huge jet, like Antonov,

00:12:40.247 --> 00:12:44.030
you don't have any room for
any drag. Like, nothing at all.

00:12:49.107 --> 00:12:51.960
Unfortunately, when
something like this happens

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:56.130
in real life, upon impact,
with the fuel and everything,

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.690
these things do tend to happen.

